GAD.reg("lesson","cma.bud.flexible",{"id":"cma.bud.flexible","title":"Flexible budgets","md":"## In plain words\n\n### The café manager who \"saved\" ₹18,000\n\nTapri's Pune café budgeted ₹1,70,000 of costs for March, for 20,000 cups. Then a water pipe burst, the café shut for ten days, and it sold only 15,000 cups. March's costs came to ₹1,52,000.\n\n> **Kesar:** ₹18,000 under budget! Give the manager a bonus.\n> **Ritu:** Per unit, please. She sold 5,000 fewer cups. The milk, tea and kulhads for those cups were never bought.\n> **Kesar:** So did she save money or not?\n> **Ritu:** Ask the budget she should have had for 15,000 cups.\n\nRitu splits the café's costs by behaviour. Rent and salaries are **fixed**: ₹60,000 whatever the cups. Milk, tea and kulhads are **variable**: ₹4 a cup. Electricity is **semi-variable**: ₹10,000 for the lights and fridges plus ₹1 a cup for the boilers. For 15,000 cups the café should have spent ₹60,000 + ₹10,000 + 15,000 × ₹5 = ₹1,45,000. It spent ₹1,52,000: ₹7,000 **more** than it should have.\n\nThe ₹1,70,000 was a **fixed budget**: prepared for one level of activity and left unchanged whatever level is actually reached. The ₹1,45,000 is a **flexible budget**: the same plan recast for the activity actually attained, because it knows which costs move with volume and which do not. Compared with the fixed budget the manager looks thrifty; compared with the flexible budget she overspent.\n\n::: analogy The road-trip fuel budget\nYou budget ₹5,000 for fuel and ₹500 for tolls for a 500-km trip, then turn back after 300 km having spent ₹3,500 on fuel. \"₹1,500 saved\"? Fuel at ₹10 a km allowed ₹3,000 for 300 km: you overspent ₹500, perhaps on the hills. The toll was the same either way. A flexible budget is the fuel allowance for the distance you actually drove.\n:::\n\n::: key\nA fixed budget is for one level of activity and does not change; a flexible budget recognises fixed, semi-variable and variable costs and changes with the level of activity attained. Judge actual costs only against the flexible budget for the actual level.\n:::\n\n## How it works\n\n### Fixed budget: one level, no change\n\nA **fixed budget** is prepared on the basis of a standard or fixed level of activity and does not change with a change in the level of activities; it shows the expected results of a responsibility centre for only one activity level, and is also known as a **static budget** (SM 15.19–15.20). Fixed budgeting is used by many service companies and for some administrative functions of manufacturing companies, such as purchasing, engineering and accounting. It is suitable for fixed expenses; when the level attained differs from the budgeted level, it becomes ineffective as a tool of cost control.\n\n**Essential conditions** for a fixed budget (SM 15.20): (1) the nature of business is not seasonal; (2) there is no impact of external factors on business activities; (3) demand for the product is certain and stable; (4) supply orders are received and issued regularly; (5) the market is normally domestic (or regular service exports); (6) no special labour or material is needed in production; (7) the supply of production inputs is regular; (8) there is a trend of price stability. These conditions are generally not all found in practice, hence a fixed budget is not suitable in business concerns.\n\n| Fixed budget: merits | Fixed budget: demerits |\n|---|---|\n| 1. Very simple to understand | 1. Does not suit a dynamic organisation and may give misleading results: a poor or good performance may remain unnoticed |\n| 2. Less time consuming | 2. Not suitable for a long period |\n| | 3. Unsuitable when business conditions are changing constantly |\n| | 4. Accurate estimates are not possible |\n\n### Flexible budget: a series of fixed budgets\n\n::: definition Flexible budget\nA budget which, by recognising the difference in behaviour between fixed and variable costs in relation to fluctuations in output, turnover, or other variable factors, is designed to change appropriately with such fluctuations. CIMA: \"a budget which, by recognizing the difference between fixed, semi-variable and variable costs is designed to change in relation to the level of activity attained.\" (SM 15.21)\n:::\n\nUnlike a static budget, a flexible budget shows the expected results of a responsibility centre for different activity levels: it can be viewed as ==a series of static budgets for different levels of activity==. It is especially useful in estimating and controlling factory costs and operating expenses, and more realistic because it considers how revenue and cost behave at different levels (SM 15.21). To prepare one, expenses are classified as (i) fixed, (ii) variable and (iii) semi-variable, and the semi-variable expenses are further segregated into fixed and variable parts ([[cma.intro.behaviour|cost behaviour]]).\n\nFlexible budgeting may be resorted to (SM 15.21): (i) in a new business venture, where demand is hard to forecast accurately; (ii) where the business depends on the fluctuations of nature (a wool trader sells plenty below freezing point and much less in warm weather); (iii) in a labour-intensive industry where production depends on the availability of labour.\n\n| Flexible budget: merits (SM 15.22) | Flexible budget: demerits (SM 15.22) |\n|---|---|\n| 1. Sales, costs and profit can be calculated easily at various levels of capacity | 1. Possible only with a proper accounting system and perfect knowledge of the factors of production and business circumstances |\n| 2. Adjustment to changed business conditions is very simple | 2. Requires a system of standard costing |\n| 3. Helps decide the production level, showing budgeted costs and sales at each level, so management can pick the level that earns the profit predetermined by the owners | 3. Very expensive and labour oriented |\n| 4. Shows the quantity to be produced to earn a determined profit | |\n\n### Preparing one, step by step\n\n::: diagram Preparing a flexible budget\n{\"type\": \"flow\", \"w\": 440, \"nodes\": [{\"id\": \"a\", \"kind\": \"start\", \"label\": \"List every cost\\nat the base level\"}, {\"id\": \"b\", \"kind\": \"decision\", \"label\": \"How does its\\ntotal move?\"}, {\"id\": \"f\", \"kind\": \"process\", \"label\": \"Stays the same:\\nfixed\"}, {\"id\": \"v\", \"kind\": \"process\", \"label\": \"In proportion:\\nvariable rate\"}, {\"id\": \"s\", \"kind\": \"process\", \"label\": \"Not in proportion:\\nsplit it\"}, {\"id\": \"d\", \"kind\": \"process\", \"label\": \"Apply the question's\\nprice and step changes\"}, {\"id\": \"e\", \"kind\": \"process\", \"label\": \"Fixed + rate × activity\\nat each level\"}, {\"id\": \"z\", \"kind\": \"end\", \"label\": \"Compare actual with\\nthe actual level's budget\"}], \"edges\": [[\"a\", \"b\"], [\"b\", \"f\"], [\"b\", \"v\"], [\"b\", \"s\"], [\"f\", \"d\"], [\"v\", \"d\"], [\"s\", \"d\"], [\"d\", \"e\"], [\"e\", \"z\"]], \"caption\": \"Classify, split, adjust, compute, compare. A cost whose total stays the same is fixed; one that rises in proportion is variable; one that rises but not in proportion is semi-variable, split into its fixed and variable parts by the high and low points method.\"}\n:::\n\nThe split of a semi-variable cost uses the **high and low points method** (SM 15.28): the variable cost per unit is the change in the expense divided by the change in output, and the fixed part is what remains of the total at either level. Then watch the question for three things ICAI likes to add: a price or cost rate that changes at certain levels, a fixed cost that steps up beyond a capacity level (an extra supervisor, more advertising), and a semi-variable cost that rises by a percentage within a band of activity (SM 15.23).\n\n::: lab budget {\"mode\":\"flexible\"}\nStep through it yourself: one cost line per frame across the levels, each split shown, then totals, cost per unit and the cost line on a chart.\n:::\n\n### The café on one chart\n\n::: diagram Café costs in March: fixed budget against flexible budget\n{\"type\": \"plot\", \"h\": 320, \"x\": [0, 25000], \"y\": [0, 200000], \"legend\": \"outside\",\n \"xlabel\": \"cups sold in the month\", \"ylabel\": \"cost (₹)\",\n \"xticks\": [[0, \"0\"], [5000, \"5,000\"], [10000, \"10,000\"], [15000, \"15,000\"], [20000, \"20,000\"], [25000, \"25,000\"]],\n \"yticks\": [[0, \"0\"], [50000, \"50,000\"], [100000, \"1,00,000\"], [145000, \"1,45,000\"], [170000, \"1,70,000\"], [200000, \"2,00,000\"]],\n \"series\": [\n  {\"label\": \"flexible budget: 70,000 + 5 × cups\", \"fn\": \"70000+5*x\", \"color\": \"a\"},\n  {\"label\": \"fixed budget: 1,70,000\", \"fn\": \"170000\", \"color\": \"d\", \"dash\": true},\n  {\"pts\": [[15000, 0], [15000, 152000]], \"color\": \"mute\", \"dash\": true, \"legend\": false}\n ],\n \"points\": [{\"x\": 15000, \"y\": 152000, \"label\": \"actual 1,52,000\", \"pos\": \"left\"}, {\"x\": 15000, \"y\": 145000, \"label\": \"allowed 1,45,000\", \"pos\": \"right\"}, {\"x\": 20000, \"y\": 170000}],\n \"caption\": \"Blue: the cost the café should incur at each level; orange dashed: the fixed budget, the same whatever the cups. The lines meet at the budgeted 20,000 cups. At 15,000 cups the actual cost sits ₹18,000 below the orange line but ₹7,000 above the blue one.\"}\n:::\n\nThe same chart explains a classic trap. At 20,000 cups the budget works out to ₹1,70,000 ÷ 20,000 = ₹8.50 a cup. Applying ₹8.50 to 15,000 cups gives ₹1,27,500, far below the correct ₹1,45,000, because the fixed ₹70,000 does not shrink with the cups: the cost per unit falls as activity rises. ICAI's Illustration 1 makes the point with a recovery rate per hour of ₹0.61 at 70% and ₹0.50 at 100% (SM 15.23–15.24).\n\n## Formats and formulas\n\n*Reproduce the format from memory; ICAI's suggested answers show every level side by side, the fixed portions worked out in notes.*\n\n::: formula Flexible budget allowance\n$$\\text{Allowance} = F + v \\times A$$\nF = fixed cost (including the fixed part of semi-variable costs); v = variable cost per unit of activity; A = activity attained, in units, hours, or sales value (for costs given as a percentage of sales).\n:::\n\n::: formula High and low points method\n$$v = \\frac{\\text{Change in expense}}{\\text{Change in output}} \\qquad F = \\text{Total expense} - v \\times \\text{Output}$$\nv = variable cost per unit; F = fixed cost. Use two levels at which the fixed cost is the same; the fixed part comes out equal at both.\n:::\n\n::: definition Fixed budget\nCIMA: \"a budget designed to remain unchanged irrespective of the level of activity actually attained.\" (SM 15.19)\n:::\n\n| Flexible budget for … | 60% | 80% | 100% |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Output (units) | xxx | xxx | xxx |\n| **Sales (A)** | **xxx** | **xxx** | **xxx** |\n| Variable costs, item by item | xxx | xxx | xxx |\n| Variable part of semi-variable costs | xxx | xxx | xxx |\n| **Total variable cost (B)** | **xxx** | **xxx** | **xxx** |\n| **Contribution (A − B)** | **xxx** | **xxx** | **xxx** |\n| Fixed costs, item by item | xxx | xxx | xxx |\n| Fixed part of semi-variable costs | xxx | xxx | xxx |\n| Step costs at the level | xxx | xxx | xxx |\n| **Total fixed cost (C)** | **xxx** | **xxx** | **xxx** |\n| **Profit (A − B − C)** | **xxx** | **xxx** | **xxx** |\n\nWhen only costs are asked (\"flexible budget showing the total cost\"), stop at total cost (B + C); ICAI often adds the cost per unit or per hour at each level.\n\n## Worked examples\n\n:::: example Cold brew: the night shift and the export order\nTapri's cold-brew plant can bottle 1,00,000 bottles a year; it now works at 60%. Ritu is preparing the budget for 60%, 80% and 100% of capacity:\n\n1. The domestic market takes at most 70,000 bottles at ₹120 each. Bottles beyond that can only be exported, at ₹105 each, with export packing and freight of ₹7 a bottle.\n\n2. Direct material costs ₹40 a bottle at every level.\n\n3. The day shift can bottle 70,000 bottles at a direct labour cost of ₹15 a bottle. Bottles beyond that come from a night shift, whose labour is paid 20% more, and which needs a night supervisor at ₹1,50,000 a year.\n\n4. Factory overheads were ₹14,40,000 in a year when the plant made 50,000 bottles and ₹16,80,000 in a year when it made 70,000; the fixed part is unchanged.\n\n5. Administration and selling overheads are ₹6,00,000 a year (fixed), plus a commission of 5% on domestic sales.\n\n**Required:**\n\n(i) Prepare a flexible budget showing the contribution and profit at 60%, 80% and 100% capacity.\n\n(ii) Comment on the level at which the plant should work, and state the smallest night-shift output that is worth running.\n\n::: answer Show the working\n**Working notes**\n\n1. Factory overheads (high and low points): variable = (₹16,80,000 − ₹14,40,000) ÷ (70,000 − 50,000) = **₹12 a bottle**; fixed = ₹14,40,000 − 50,000 × ₹12 = **₹8,40,000**.\n\n2. Bottles: 60% = 60,000, all domestic; 80% = 70,000 domestic + 10,000 export; 100% = 70,000 domestic + 30,000 export.\n\n3. Labour: day shift ₹15; night shift ₹15 × 120% = **₹18 a bottle**, on the bottles beyond 70,000.\n\n4. Commission: 5% × ₹120 = ₹6 a domestic bottle; none on exports.\n\n**(i) Flexible budget**\n\n| Particulars | 60% (₹) | 80% (₹) | 100% (₹) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Bottles | 60,000 | 80,000 | 1,00,000 |\n| Domestic sales @ ₹120 | 72,00,000 | 84,00,000 | 84,00,000 |\n| Export sales @ ₹105 | – | 10,50,000 | 31,50,000 |\n| **Sales (A)** | **72,00,000** | **94,50,000** | **1,15,50,000** |\n| Direct material @ ₹40 | 24,00,000 | 32,00,000 | 40,00,000 |\n| Direct labour, day shift @ ₹15 | 9,00,000 | 10,50,000 | 10,50,000 |\n| Direct labour, night shift @ ₹18 | – | 1,80,000 | 5,40,000 |\n| Variable factory overheads @ ₹12 | 7,20,000 | 9,60,000 | 12,00,000 |\n| Commission (5% of domestic sales) | 3,60,000 | 4,20,000 | 4,20,000 |\n| Export packing and freight @ ₹7 | – | 70,000 | 2,10,000 |\n| **Total variable cost (B)** | **43,80,000** | **58,80,000** | **74,20,000** |\n| **Contribution (A − B)** | **28,20,000** | **35,70,000** | **41,30,000** |\n| Fixed factory overheads (WN 1) | 8,40,000 | 8,40,000 | 8,40,000 |\n| Administration and selling (fixed) | 6,00,000 | 6,00,000 | 6,00,000 |\n| Night supervisor | – | 1,50,000 | 1,50,000 |\n| **Fixed cost (C)** | **14,40,000** | **15,90,000** | **15,90,000** |\n| **Profit (A − B − C)** | **13,80,000** | **19,80,000** | **25,40,000** |\n| Total cost per bottle (B + C) | 97.00 | 93.38 | 90.10 |\n\n**(ii) Comment.** Profit is highest at **100%** (₹25,40,000). A domestic bottle contributes ₹120 − ₹40 − ₹15 − ₹12 − ₹6 = ₹47; an export bottle from the night shift only ₹105 − ₹40 − ₹18 − ₹12 − ₹7 = ₹28. The lower price applies only to the extra bottles, not to the whole output, so every export bottle still adds ₹28, and the move from 80% to 100% adds 20,000 × ₹28 = ₹5,60,000. The night shift must first cover its supervisor: ₹1,50,000 ÷ ₹28 = 5,357.1, so it is worth running only if it makes at least **5,358 bottles**. The plant should work at 100%, provided the export buyer takes 30,000 bottles at ₹105.\n:::\n::::\n\n::: lab budget {\"mode\":\"flexible\",\"unit\":\"units\",\"cap\":120000,\"base\":96000,\"levels\":\"60%, 80%, 100%\",\"price\":120,\"lines\":\"Direct variable cost: variable 20\\nFactory overheads: semi 8,40,000, 20% fixed\\nAdministrative overheads: fixed 20,60,000\\nSales commission: 2% of sales\\nTransportation: step 4000 per 4000\"}\nHere is September 2024 Q3(b) at 60%, 80% and 100%, with a semi-variable split, a commission on sales and a step cost for transport.\n:::\n\n:::: example Three cafés, one league table\nKesar ranks the managers of Tapri's three Indore cafés every month by \"actual cost against budget\". Each café's monthly cost budget is built from the same cost behaviour, which Ritu has established:\n\n- Milk, tea, sugar and kulhads: ₹6.00 a cup (variable).\n- Electricity and gas: ₹8,000 a month plus ₹0.50 a cup (semi-variable).\n- Rent and salaries: fixed, different for each café.\n\n| June | Rajwada | Vijay Nagar | Palasia |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Budgeted cups | 30,000 | 24,000 | 18,000 |\n| Cups actually sold | 36,000 | 20,000 | 18,500 |\n| Rent and salaries (₹) | 1,20,000 | 1,00,000 | 90,000 |\n| Actual total cost (₹) | 3,54,000 | 2,46,000 | 2,20,000 |\n\nPalasia's actual cost includes ₹6,000 for a boiler overhaul ordered and approved by head office. Kesar's June table puts Vijay Nagar first, Palasia second and Rajwada last, and he plans to move the Rajwada manager to a smaller café.\n\n**Required:**\n\n(i) Prepare each café's fixed budget for June and its flexible budget for the cups actually sold.\n\n(ii) Compare actual costs with both budgets and rank the managers on the comparison that measures their performance.\n\n(iii) Comment on Kesar's league table and his plan.\n\n::: answer Show the working\n**(i) Budgets** (fixed part of electricity ₹8,000; variable cost ₹6.00 + ₹0.50 = ₹6.50 a cup)\n\n| Particulars | Rajwada (₹) | Vijay Nagar (₹) | Palasia (₹) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Rent and salaries | 1,20,000 | 1,00,000 | 90,000 |\n| Electricity and gas, fixed part | 8,000 | 8,000 | 8,000 |\n| **Fixed costs** | **1,28,000** | **1,08,000** | **98,000** |\n| Fixed budget: + ₹6.50 × budgeted cups | 1,95,000 | 1,56,000 | 1,17,000 |\n| **Fixed budget** | **3,23,000** | **2,64,000** | **2,15,000** |\n| Flexible budget: + ₹6.50 × actual cups | 2,34,000 | 1,30,000 | 1,20,250 |\n| **Flexible budget** | **3,62,000** | **2,38,000** | **2,18,250** |\n\n**(ii) Comparison**\n\n| Particulars | Rajwada (₹) | Vijay Nagar (₹) | Palasia (₹) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Actual cost | 3,54,000 | 2,46,000 | 2,20,000 |\n| Over / (under) the fixed budget | 31,000 | (18,000) | 5,000 |\n| Over / (under) the flexible budget | (8,000) | 8,000 | 1,750 |\n| Less: head office's boiler overhaul | – | – | (6,000) |\n| **Controllable over / (under) the flexible budget** | **(8,000)** | **8,000** | **(4,250)** |\n| As % of the flexible budget | (2.21%) | 3.36% | (1.95%) |\n| **Rank** | **I** | **III** | **II** |\n\n**(iii) Comment.** Kesar's table compares each café's actual cost with a budget for a different number of cups, so it rewards lower sales: Vijay Nagar \"saves\" ₹18,000 only because it sold 4,000 fewer cups, and Rajwada \"overspends\" ₹31,000 only because it sold 6,000 more. Flexed to the cups actually sold, Rajwada spent ₹8,000 less than its allowance and is the best performer, while Vijay Nagar overspent by ₹8,000 and is the worst. Palasia's boiler overhaul was a head-office decision, so it is excluded from the manager's performance. The plan to move the Rajwada manager should be dropped; Vijay Nagar's overspending, on lower sales, is what needs explaining.\n:::\n::::\n\n::: lab budget {\"mode\":\"flexible\",\"unit\":\"units\",\"cap\":0,\"base\":8000,\"levels\":\"6000 8000 10000\",\"price\":0,\"actLevel\":6000,\"lines\":\"Salary of foreman: fixed 1000, actual 1000\\nIndirect labour: variable 720 in total, actual 600\\nIndirect material: variable 800 in total, actual 700\\nRepairs and maintenance: semi 600, 650 at 10000, actual 600\\nPower: semi 800, 875 at 10000, actual 740\\nTools consumed: variable 320 in total, actual 300\\nRates and taxes: fixed 150, actual 150\\nDepreciation: fixed 800, actual 800\\nInsurance: fixed 100, actual 100\"}\nThe same control check on Study Material Illustration 3: the budget flexed to 6,000 units and set against the actual costs, line by line.\n:::\n\n:::: example Rusks: a second oven line in the past data\nTapri's rusk unit sells its rusks at ₹90 a carton. Its production overheads in the last three years were:\n\n| Year | Output (cartons) | Production overheads (₹) |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| 2023-24 | 40,000 | 9,20,000 |\n| 2024-25 | 50,000 | 10,40,000 |\n| 2025-26 | 65,000 | 13,70,000 |\n\nA second oven line was commissioned at the start of 2025-26, adding ₹1,50,000 a year to fixed production overheads; with it the unit can bake 1,00,000 cartons a year. Other costs in 2025-26: direct materials ₹30 and direct labour ₹15 a carton; selling overheads ₹2,00,000 a year plus a commission of 5% on sales; administrative overheads ₹2,40,000 a year.\n\nFor 2026-27 the selling price is unchanged and these increases are expected: direct materials 10%, direct labour 8%, variable production overheads 5%, all fixed overheads 10%.\n\n**Required:**\n\n(i) Segregate the production overheads into fixed and variable elements.\n\n(ii) Prepare a flexible budget for 2026-27 at 60,000, 70,000 and 80,000 cartons, showing the contribution and profit.\n\n(iii) Kesar wants a profit of ₹10,00,000 in 2026-27. Calculate the output needed, and state whether the unit can produce it.\n\n::: answer Show the working\n**(i) Segregation.** Use two years with the same fixed overheads: 2023-24 and 2024-25 (2025-26 includes the second line).\n\nVariable = (₹10,40,000 − ₹9,20,000) ÷ (50,000 − 40,000) = **₹12 a carton**; fixed = ₹9,20,000 − 40,000 × ₹12 = **₹4,40,000**.\n\nCheck 2025-26: ₹4,40,000 + ₹1,50,000 + 65,000 × ₹12 = ₹13,70,000. Taking 2023-24 and 2025-26 instead would give (₹13,70,000 − ₹9,20,000) ÷ 25,000 = ₹18 a carton, wrongly treating the new line's fixed cost as variable.\n\n**Working note: rates for 2026-27**\n\n| Item | Working | 2026-27 |\n|---|---|---:|\n| Direct materials | ₹30 × 110% | ₹33.00 a carton |\n| Direct labour | ₹15 × 108% | ₹16.20 a carton |\n| Variable production OH | ₹12 × 105% | ₹12.60 a carton |\n| Commission | 5% × ₹90 | ₹4.50 a carton |\n| Fixed production OH | (₹4,40,000 + ₹1,50,000) × 110% | ₹6,49,000 |\n| Fixed selling OH | ₹2,00,000 × 110% | ₹2,20,000 |\n| Administrative OH | ₹2,40,000 × 110% | ₹2,64,000 |\n\nOH: overheads. Variable cost ₹66.30 a carton; contribution ₹90 − ₹66.30 = ₹23.70 a carton; fixed cost ₹11,33,000.\n\n**(ii) Flexible budget for 2026-27**\n\n| Particulars | 60,000 (₹) | 70,000 (₹) | 80,000 (₹) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| **Sales @ ₹90 (A)** | **54,00,000** | **63,00,000** | **72,00,000** |\n| Direct materials @ ₹33 | 19,80,000 | 23,10,000 | 26,40,000 |\n| Direct labour @ ₹16.20 | 9,72,000 | 11,34,000 | 12,96,000 |\n| Variable production OH @ ₹12.60 | 7,56,000 | 8,82,000 | 10,08,000 |\n| Commission @ ₹4.50 | 2,70,000 | 3,15,000 | 3,60,000 |\n| **Variable cost (B)** | **39,78,000** | **46,41,000** | **53,04,000** |\n| **Contribution (A − B)** | **14,22,000** | **16,59,000** | **18,96,000** |\n| Fixed production OH | 6,49,000 | 6,49,000 | 6,49,000 |\n| Fixed selling OH | 2,20,000 | 2,20,000 | 2,20,000 |\n| Administrative OH | 2,64,000 | 2,64,000 | 2,64,000 |\n| **Fixed cost (C)** | **11,33,000** | **11,33,000** | **11,33,000** |\n| **Profit (A − B − C)** | **2,89,000** | **5,26,000** | **7,63,000** |\n\n**(iii) Output for a profit of ₹10,00,000** = (fixed cost + required profit) ÷ contribution per carton = (₹11,33,000 + ₹10,00,000) ÷ ₹23.70 = **90,000 cartons**. That is within the capacity of 1,00,000 cartons, so the unit can produce it, provided the market takes 90,000 cartons at ₹90. This is the use of a flexible budget that ICAI lists last: it shows the quantity to be produced to earn a determined profit.\n:::\n::::\n\n## Under the hood\n\n### The flexible budget at standard cost\n\nA flexible budget can also be drawn up at **standard cost** for the actual output and set beside the master budget and the actual results, item by item: the master budget for the planned output, the flexible budget for the actual output, the actual costs, and the variance between the last two (the chapter's Practical Problem 6, SM 15.79 and 15.92). The fixed overhead expenditure variance is then budgeted fixed overhead minus actual fixed overhead. The variance analysis itself is in [[cma.std.voh|variable overhead variances]], [[cma.std.foh|fixed overhead variances]] and [[cma.std.recon|reconciling budgeted and actual profit]].\n\n### Step costs: the bus problem\n\nICAI's favourite flexible budget outside the factory is an outing: students or trainees, buses or batches with a maximum capacity, and costs per student, per bus and per trip. Costs per head are variable; costs per bus are semi-variable in steps (buses = students ÷ seats, rounded **up**); a lump sum is fixed. The average cost per head falls as numbers rise, but jumps whenever a new bus is hired. May 2025 Q5(c) (seats reserved for teachers, and block fees that change with the group size), MTP May 2024 Series II Q3(a) and the case scenario in MTP May 2026 Series II all use it.\n\n### Revenue budgets are flexible too\n\nThe chapter's case scenario prepares a public sector undertaking's **revenue budget** as a flexible budget: sales volume grown by a percentage, raw material, power and maintenance moved with volume, wages linked to a wage agreement and an index, prices raised only if a price index crosses its trigger (SM 15.81–15.83, 15.96–15.98). The method is the same: decide what each item moves with, then move it.\n\n### \"In the absence of information\"\n\nWhen the capacity rises beyond the present level and the question says nothing, ICAI assumes the fixed costs stay the same, and says so in a note: \"However, in practice some of these costs may change if present capacity is exceeded\" (Illustration 2, SM 15.26). Write the same assumption in your answer.\n\n## Exam angle\n\n::: exam Where flexible budgets appeared, May 2024 to September 2026\nIn the new-scheme ICAI documents read for this lesson, flexible budgets were asked 17 times in 15 documents.\n\n- **Profit or cost at several capacity levels** (the core numerical): May 2024 Q3(a) (7 marks: 60%, 70%, 90%, price falls, material rises, extra advertising above 80%); September 2024 Q3(b) (6 marks: total cost at 60%, 80%, 100%, transport per truck); MTP May 2026 Series I Q2(b) (6 marks: expense budget at two outputs); MTP September 2025 Series II Q3(b) (6 marks: before and after marketing efforts); RTP January 2025 Q18 (administration, selling and distribution costs at four levels).\n- **From past years' costs to a new level with cost increases**: MTP September 2024 Series II Q5(b) (7 marks), the chapter's Practical Problem 4 pattern.\n- **Step costs in a service setting**: May 2025 Q5(c) (7 marks, a college trip); MTP May 2024 Series II Q3(a) (10 marks, a school picnic with a break-even fee); MTP May 2026 Series II MCQs 6–10 (five MCQs on a training workshop).\n- **Harder variants**: RTP May 2025 Q19 (overtime premium above a volume and a learning effect); RTP May 2024 Q12 (the revenue budget case).\n- **MCQs, 2 marks**: MTP May 2024 Series I MCQ 4 and MTP September 2026 Series I MCQ 15 (the same: overheads at 60% from the figures at 50% and 75%).\n- **Theory, 4 or 5 marks**: demerits of fixed and flexible budgets, MTP September 2024 Series II Q6(c); suitability for a flexible budget, MTP January 2026 Series II Q4(c); \"which budgeting system would you recommend\", MTP September 2025 Series II Q6(b); differences between fixed and flexible budgets, RTP September 2025 Q18(c).\n:::\n\n::: exam The traps ICAI sets\n- A transport, bus or batch cost per vehicle is a step cost: round the number of vehicles **up** (unless the loads divide exactly, as in September 2024, where it became a variable ₹1 a unit).\n- A fixed cost that starts \"above 80%\" or \"beyond 90%\" is added only at the levels above it.\n- The fixed portion of a semi-variable cost is worked out at the base level and held constant; percentage increases are applied to the variable rate and to the fixed total separately.\n- \"Profit 20% on sales\" is 25% on cost.\n- Compare actual cost with the flexible budget for the **actual** level; a fixed budget shows \"savings\" that are only lower volume.\n:::\n\n::: trick Fixed cost survives, variable cost travels\nWhatever the level: fixed cost stays as a total, variable cost moves as a rate × level. So total cost per unit always falls as the level rises (until a step cost or a price change intervenes). Condition: costs linear within the range, with step and price changes added separately.\n:::\n\n## Answer frame\n\n### \"Distinguish between fixed and flexible budgets\"\n\nICAI's five points, in order (SM 15.22–15.23):\n\n| Point | Fixed budget | Flexible budget |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Change | Does not change with the actual volume achieved: rigid or inflexible | Can be re-cast for the activity level achieved: not rigid |\n| 2. Levels | One level of activity, one set of conditions; assumes no change, which is unrealistic | Various budgets for different levels of activity |\n| 3. Variance analysis | All costs related to one level, so it does not give useful information | Each cost analysed by behaviour, so it gives useful information |\n| 4. Cost and price | Cost ascertainment and price fixation mislead when budgeted and actual levels differ significantly | Facilitates cost ascertainment, price fixation and quotations |\n| 5. Comparison | Comparing actual with budget is meaningless when activity levels differ | A meaningful basis of comparison of actual with budget |\n\n### \"Explain briefly the concept of flexible budget\"\n\n1. The definition (the behaviour of fixed and variable costs; CIMA's wording with semi-variable costs).\n2. Unlike a static budget: results for different activity levels; a series of static budgets.\n3. Costs classified as fixed, variable and semi-variable, the last split into fixed and variable.\n4. Where it is used: new ventures, businesses dependent on nature, labour-intensive industries.\n5. A three-line numerical illustration (the café).\n\n### \"Which budgeting system would you recommend?\" (actuals far above a fixed budget)\n\nICAI's suggested answer (MTP September 2025 Series II Q6(b)): recommend a **flexible budget**; define it (both definitions); state that unlike static budgets it shows expected results for different activity levels; then list its advantages: sales, costs and profit calculated easily at various levels; simple adjustment to changes in business conditions; helps select the production level that earns the predetermined profit; shows the quantity to be produced to earn a determined profit.\n\n### \"Suitability for flexible budget\" (SM 15.21–15.22)\n\n1. Seasonal fluctuations in sales and/or production, for example the soft drinks industry.\n2. A company which keeps introducing new products or changes the design of its products frequently.\n3. Industries engaged in make-to-order business, like ship building.\n4. An industry influenced by changes in fashion.\n5. General changes in sales.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n::: mistake Hall of Shame: the league table that punished the busiest café\nComparing Rajwada's actual ₹3,54,000 with the ₹3,23,000 budgeted for 30,000 cups and calling the ₹31,000 difference an overspend. **Fix:** flex the budget to the 36,000 cups actually sold (₹3,62,000): the café stayed ₹8,000 within it. The same error works the other way: Vijay Nagar's \"saving\" of ₹18,000 against its fixed budget is only lower volume.\n:::\n\n::: mistake The budget rate per unit carried to another level\n₹8.50 a cup at 20,000 cups applied to 15,000 cups gives ₹1,27,500. **Fix:** fixed costs do not shrink: allowance = fixed ₹70,000 + ₹5 × 15,000 = ₹1,45,000.\n:::\n\n::: mistake Semi-variable costs treated as wholly variable or wholly fixed\nTreating the cold-brew plant's ₹16,80,000 of factory overheads at 70,000 bottles as all variable (₹24 a bottle) or all fixed. **Fix:** split it first, by the percentage the question gives or by the high and low points method (here ₹8,40,000 fixed and ₹12 a bottle); flex only the variable part. And pick two levels with the same fixed cost: the rusk unit's 2025-26 figure includes a second oven line.\n:::\n\n::: mistake 8.33 vans\nA fraction of a van, a bus or a batch: 1,000 crates ÷ 120 = 8.33 vans. **Fix:** vehicles and batches are hired whole: round up (9 vans), and cost the step.\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n::: quiz Check yourself\nQ: A bakery's budgeted overheads are ₹2,40,000 at 40% capacity and ₹3,30,000 at 70%, with the same fixed cost at both levels. Its manager has been given an overhead allowance of ₹3,00,000 for next month. At what capacity level (in %) would that allowance be exactly right?\n= 60\n> Variable cost per 1% of capacity = ₹90,000 ÷ 30 = ₹3,000; fixed = ₹2,40,000 − 40 × ₹3,000 = ₹1,20,000. ₹3,00,000 = ₹1,20,000 + ₹3,000 × level, so level = ₹1,80,000 ÷ ₹3,000 = 60%. Dividing ₹3,00,000 by the ₹6,000 per 1% at 40% (₹2,40,000 ÷ 40) gives 50%, as if the fixed cost moved with activity.\n\nQ: Case scenario. Tapri's samosa factory sends crates to its cafés in vans that carry 120 crates each, at ₹1,800 a van trip. Loading labour costs ₹5 a crate, and the dispatch clerk's salary is ₹12,000 a month. The monthly flexible budget is ₹33,200 for 1,000 crates. By how much does the budget rise if the factory dispatches 1,100 crates?\n- [ ] ₹3,320\n- [ ] ₹500\n- [x] ₹2,300\n- [ ] ₹1,800\n> Vans are hired whole: 1,000 crates need 9 trips (8.33 rounded up) and 1,100 crates need 10 (9.17 rounded up). The rise is one more trip, ₹1,800, plus loading 100 × ₹5 = ₹500: ₹2,300. ₹3,320 treats the whole ₹33,200 as variable (10% more); ₹500 forgets the extra van; ₹1,800 forgets the loading.\n\nQ: A unit's budget at 8,000 machine hours is ₹96,000: ₹48,000 fixed and ₹6 an hour variable. It actually works 6,000 hours. What is the correct budget allowance?\n- [ ] ₹72,000\n- [x] ₹84,000\n- [ ] ₹96,000\n- [ ] ₹36,000\n> Fixed ₹48,000 + 6,000 × ₹6 = ₹84,000. ₹72,000 applies the ₹12 rate per hour at 8,000 hours (₹96,000 ÷ 8,000) to 6,000 hours, as if the fixed cost shrank; ₹96,000 is the fixed budget; ₹36,000 forgets the fixed cost.\n\nQ: For which of these businesses is a flexible budget most suitable?\n- [x] A soft-drinks maker whose sales peak every summer\n- [ ] A firm with certain and stable demand, regular supplies and stable prices\n- [ ] A department all of whose costs are fixed\n- [ ] A firm that never changes its products and sells only in a steady domestic market\n> Seasonal fluctuation in sales or production is the first case in ICAI's list of suitability. The other three describe the conditions under which a fixed budget works.\n\nQ: Which of these are ICAI's essential conditions for a fixed budget? Select all that apply.\n- [x] The nature of the business is not seasonal\n- [x] The demand for the product is certain and stable\n- [ ] The company keeps introducing new products\n- [x] There is a trend of price stability\n- [ ] The business is make-to-order, like ship building\n> The fixed budget needs a non-seasonal business, stable demand, regular supply orders and inputs, no special labour or material, a mainly domestic market, no external impact and stable prices. New products and make-to-order work are reasons for a flexible budget.\n:::\n\n## Recap\n\n- Fixed (static) budget: one level of activity, unchanged whatever is attained; for stable, non-seasonal businesses and fixed expenses.\n- Flexible budget (CIMA): recognises fixed, semi-variable and variable costs and changes with the level of activity attained; a series of static budgets.\n- Budgeted cost = fixed cost + variable cost per unit × activity; split semi-variables first (high and low points: change in expense ÷ change in output).\n- Add the question's steps: price changes at a level, extra fixed costs beyond a capacity, vehicles or batches rounded up.\n- Judge performance against the flexible budget for the actual level: a fixed budget's \"saving\" may be only lower volume.\n- Never carry a cost per unit from one level to another: fixed costs do not shrink.\n- Fixed vs flexible: change, levels, variance analysis, cost and price, comparison.\n- Suitable where sales are seasonal, products or designs keep changing, work is made to order, fashion rules, or sales change generally. (Fuel for the distance you actually drove.)"});
